  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nJet-induced ascent along the western periphery of a sagging OH\nRiver Valley trough in combination with a significantly moist\nenvironment and enough diurnal heating to foster showers and\nthunderstorms through this evening. The focus for activity is\neast of I-77 in OH as satellite imagery highlights an area of\nweak convergence near the OH/WV border currently that will shift\nNE through tonight.\n\nFlooding remains the highest risk factor due to: 1) PWAT values\napproaching 1.7\" or around 90th percentile amounts (dry slotting\naround upper low as reduced amounts compared to 12z sounding);\n2) warm cloud layer aiding warm rain processes (13.5kft freezing\nheight); 3) storm motion not too far off parallel from surface\nboundary potentially allowing for storm training; and 4)\npotential surface heating within cloud breaks to improve\nbuoyancy and help rainfall rates (ongoing storms suggests\n2-3\"/hr rates possible, higher can't be ruled out). The most\nsusceptible areas are those previously receiving rain,\nspecifically a narrow swath from north central Greene County\n(PA) through central Allegheny County (PA) that saw anywhere\nfrom 1-2.5\" already today. The biggest uncertainty lies in\nwhether enough buoyancy and convergence develop to result in\nmore isolated vs. more widespread storm coverage.\n\nOther hazards to monitor but are of lower probability is\ndamaging wind and tornadoes. For damaging wind, the threat is\ntied to deep enough convection to tap into a layer of drier air\nbetween 600-400mb that may be enough for downburst potential;\nhowever, latest ACARS a more bearish on this dry layer which\nsuggests the limited threat is even lower. For tornadoes, 0-3km\nhodograph curvature insinuates potential for right-movers (think\nmotion NE at 45 degrees) to maximize sfc vorticity and create\ngenerally isolated, weak tornadoes. Hi-res guidance suggests any\nsuch occurrence is favored SE of Pittsburgh where low-level\ncurvature and afternoon heating may be best maximized prior to\nthe convergent line arriving.\n\nLoss of heating plus eastward movement of the upper trough\nfavors downward trends of convective activity after 10pm with\nall risks ending by midnight (if not, sooner).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nShortwave ridging and surface high pressure favors a drying\ntrend Monday into Tuesday, though enough moisture may linger to\noffer low probability afternoon thunderstorms that develop at\npeak diurnal heating.\n\nLong-range pattern favors renewed shortwave trough movement\nthrough the Ohio River Valley but with varying depths and\ntiming. If the pattern features a weaker trough farther north, \nnear daily precipitation chances may better be accompanied by \nsevere/flood hazards due to better moisture influx and limited \nairmass changing. A deeper trough pattern would result in less \nfrequent precipitation chances that limits the days of potential\nimpacts. It remains best to monitor forecast trends for\nWednesday into next weekend to better understand potential storm\ntiming and their associated risks.",
